% 48e-self-hosting-strtab.weft — Stage 8f: the string interner, written in C,
% compiled by kcc itself, and linked back into the compiler.

\chapter{Self-Hosting: The String Interner in C}
\label{ch:self-hosting-strtab}

With the arena, the vector, the string primitives, and the hash table all
ported, the natural next piece is the one that sits directly on top of the last
two: the string interner of Chapter~\ref{ch:data-structures}.  Interning is how
\kcc{} guarantees that each distinct identifier or keyword text is stored
exactly once, so two names can be compared by pointer instead of byte by
byte---the property the symbol table relies on.  The interner is a thin layer,
just two routines and a global handle, but it is a satisfying rung because
\emph{every} function it calls is now itself C \kcc{} compiled: the hash table
(\texttt{kcc\_ht\_init}/\texttt{get}/\texttt{put}), the arena
(\texttt{kcc\_arena\_alloc}), and \texttt{kcc\_memcpy}.  Porting it means a name
lookup runs entirely through code the compiler produced.

\section{State and Contract}

The interner keeps one global, \texttt{kcc\_strtab}, a pointer to its backing
hash table, zero until \texttt{kcc\_strtab\_init} creates a 256-slot table.  As
a drop-in that global keeps the exact name and zero-initialised layout the
assembly used.  \texttt{kcc\_strtab\_intern} takes a string and length (the text
need not be NUL-terminated): if the hash table already holds it, the stored
pointer---which is the canonical copy---comes straight back; otherwise the
routine allocates \texttt{len+1} bytes from the arena, copies the text,
NUL-terminates it, records it in the table keyed by its own bytes with itself as
the value, and returns it.  Storing the copy as both key and value is what lets
a later lookup return the canonical pointer.

@l c
@d Selfhost: strtab @{
void *kcc_ht_init(unsigned long capacity);
unsigned long kcc_ht_get(void *ht, const void *key, unsigned long keylen);
void kcc_ht_put(void *ht, const void *key, unsigned long keylen,
                unsigned long value);
void *kcc_arena_alloc(long size);
void *kcc_memcpy(void *dst, const void *src, unsigned long n);

void *kcc_strtab;

void kcc_strtab_init(void) {
    kcc_strtab = kcc_ht_init(256);
}

const char *kcc_strtab_intern(const char *s, unsigned long len) {
    unsigned long found = kcc_ht_get(kcc_strtab, s, len);
    if (found) return (const char *)found;
    char *copy = (char *)kcc_arena_alloc((long)(len + 1));
    kcc_memcpy(copy, s, len);
    copy[len] = 0;
    kcc_ht_put(kcc_strtab, copy, len, (unsigned long)copy);
    return copy;
}
@}

\section{Verification}
\label{sec:selfhost-strtab-verify}

\texttt{make selfhost-strtab} runs the drop-in loop: compile
\texttt{gen/selfhost/strtab.c} with the assembly-built \kcc{}, relink a second
compiler with the hand-written \texttt{strtab.o} replaced by the kcc-compiled
\texttt{strtab\_c.o}, and re-run the code-generation suite.  Because every
identifier and keyword the lexer sees is interned through this layer, a green
run means \kcc{} compiled its own name-canonicalisation correctly.

@d Selfhost: strtab verify @{
#!/usr/bin/env bash
BASE="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)"
# make selfhost-strtab — compile kcc's string interner in C with kcc, relink,
# run the suite.
set -euo pipefail

SDK=$(xcrun --show-sdk-path)
SH=$BASE/selfhost
sources="main syscalls arena strings io hashtab vec strtab source lexer \
lexer_tables diag preprocessor parser expressions statements declarations \
types symtab conversions sema ir opt emit_arm64 emit_x86 linker codegen emit_macho dwarf driver"

echo "[1/3] compiling $BASE/selfhost/strtab.c with kcc"
$BASE/kcc -c "$SH/strtab.c" -o "$SH/strtab_c.o" >/dev/null

echo "[2/3] linking $BASE/kcc_selfhost_strtab (strtab.o -> kcc-compiled strtab_c.o)"
OBJ=""
for f in $sources; do
    if [ "$f" = "strtab" ]; then OBJ="$OBJ $SH/strtab_c.o"; else OBJ="$OBJ $BASE/src/${f}.o"; fi
done
ld -o $BASE/kcc_selfhost_strtab $OBJ -lSystem -syslibroot "$SDK" -e _main -arch arm64

echo "[3/3] running the codegen suite with the self-hosted interner"
cp $BASE/kcc "$SH/kcc_asm_backup_strtab"
cp $BASE/kcc_selfhost_strtab $BASE/kcc
set +e
bash $BASE/tests/test_codegen.sh
status=$?
cp "$SH/kcc_asm_backup_strtab" $BASE/kcc
exit $status
@}
